# Here ARE theory of 10 c++ questions 

1. What is Object Oriented Programming (OOP) in C++?
Explanation:
OOP is a programming approach where programs are built using objects that represent real-world entities.
Main principles:
Encapsulation – data protection
Inheritance – reuse of code
Polymorphism – same function, different behavior
Abstraction – hide complexity
Why useful?
Makes code modular, reusable, and easier to maintain.

2. What is the difference between struct and class?
Explanation:
Both are used to create user-defined types.
Feature	struct	class
Default access	public	private
Usage	simple data objects	complex OOP designs
Functionally, they are almost the same.

3. What is a constructor?
Explanation:
A constructor is a special function that runs automatically when an object is created.
Purpose:
Initialize variables
Allocate resources
Types:
Default constructor
Parameterized constructor
Copy constructor

4. What is a destructor?
Explanation:
A destructor runs automatically when an object goes out of scope.
Purpose:
Free memory
Release files, locks, etc.
It has the same name as the class but starts with ~.

5. What is inheritance and why is it used?
Explanation:
Inheritance allows a new class to use features of an existing class.
Benefits:
Code reuse
Easy maintenance
Logical hierarchy
Example:
Car inherits from Vehicle

6. What is polymorphism?
Explanation:
Polymorphism means one name, many forms.
Types:
Compile-time → function overloading
Run-time → function overriding using virtual
Why important?
Allows flexible and extensible code.

7. What is encapsulation?
Explanation:
Encapsulation binds data and methods together and restricts access.
How achieved?
Using private, protected, public
Benefit:
Prevents accidental data modification.

8. What is abstraction?
Explanation:
Abstraction hides implementation details and shows only essential features.
How achieved?
Abstract classes
Pure virtual functions
Example:
User knows how to drive, not how engine works.

9. What are pointers?
Explanation:
Pointers store the address of another variable.
Why used?
Dynamic memory allocation
Efficient parameter passing
Data structures like linked lists

10. What is dynamic memory allocation?
Explanation:
Memory allocated at runtime using:
new → allocate
delete → deallocate
Useful when size is unknown at compile time.

11. What is the difference between new/delete and malloc/free?
Explanation:
new/delete	malloc/free
C++ operators	C functions
Calls constructor	No constructor
Type safe	Not type safe

12. What is STL?
Explanation:
STL is a collection of ready-made data structures and algorithms.
Components:
Containers (vector, map)
Algorithms (sort, search)
Iterators
Advantage:
Saves time and increases efficiency.

13. What is a virtual function?
Explanation:
A virtual function ensures the correct function is called at runtime when using base class pointers.
Used for:
Runtime polymorphism.

14. What is the difference between == and =?
Explanation:
= assigns value
== compares values
Very common interview trick question.

15. What is const in C++?
Explanation:
const makes a variable or object read-only.
Usage:
Prevent modification
Improve code safety
Enable compiler optimizations
